      "question": {
        "sourceId": "LUX-MATH-Y9-SIMULTANEOUS-013",
        "revisionId": "LUX-MATH-Y9-SIMULTANEOUS-013-PRIORITY-CONTENT-REVIEW-20261124",
        "replacesOriginalRevisionId": "LUX-MATH-Y9-SIMULTANEOUS-013-REVIEW-2",
        "sourceTemplateId": "V2-SIMULTANEOUS-4",
        "topicId": "math-y9-simultaneous-equations",
        "stem": "For real parameters $k$ and $t$, consider $\\begin{cases}2x+3y=7\\\\(k+2)x+(2k+1)y=14+t\\end{cases}$. In the options, $I$ states when the system has **infinitely many solutions**, and $N$ states when it has **no solution**. Which gives both conditions correctly?",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "latex": "I:k=4,t\\ne7;N:k=4,t=7",
            "errorPath": "Interchange the equal and unequal right-hand-side conditions: the consistent proportional case gives infinitely many, not none."
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "latex": "I:k=3,t=7;N:k=3,t\\ne7",
            "errorPath": "Set the coefficient proportionality at k=3 even though it does not make both rows parallel."
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "latex": "I:k=4,t=7;N:k=4,t\\ne7"
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "latex": "I:k=4,t=21;N:k=4,t\\ne21",
            "errorPath": "Forget the second constant is 14+t, not t, when matching the triple of the first right-hand side."
          },
          {
            "id": "E",
            "latex": "I:k=4,t=7;N:k\\ne4,t=7",
            "errorPath": "Treat the nonsingular cases with k different from four as inconsistent, although they instead have one unique solution."
          },
          {
            "id": "F",
            "latex": "I:k=4,t=7;N:k=4,t>7",
            "errorPath": "Exclude inconsistent cases with t below seven, despite every t different from seven producing parallel distinct lines."
          }
        ],
        "correctOptionId": "C",
        "correctAnswerLatex": "I:k=4,t=7;N:k=4,t\\ne7",
        "workedSolutionSteps": [
          "Multiply the first equation by 2k+1 and the second by 3, then subtract the second resulting equation from the first: (k−4)x=7(2k+1)−3(14+t)=14k−35−3t. If k≠4, x is uniquely determined and the first original equation uniquely determines y.",
          "When k=4, the second left-hand side is 6x+9y=3(2x+3y). To describe the same line, its right-hand side 14+t must equal 21.",
          "Therefore I is k=4,t=7 and N is k=4,t≠7. All remaining cases have one solution."
        ],
        "provisionalAOFocus": "AO2/AO3 candidate: classify the full two-parameter set into unique, parallel-distinct and coincident systems.",
        "explicitDifferenceFromRoutineSource": "Requires two independent parameter conditions and a three-way solution classification instead of finding k from one ratio.",
        "unverifiedTaughtSubskill": "Parameter-dependent elimination and identical/distinct parallel equations.",
